<Layout
  title="0013. Consolidate governance publication into the website repository | Macro Evidence"
  description="Governance decision 0013: Consolidate governance publication into the website repository. Status: Accepted."
>
  <PageIntro
    kicker="Governance decision"
    title="0013. Consolidate governance publication into the website repository"
    lead="Governance decision 0013 of the Macro Evidence Governance decision series."
  />

  <section class="section policy-page">
    <div class="site-container reading policy-reading-wide">
      <article class="policy-text">
        <p class="policy-status">
          <strong>Status:</strong>&#32;Accepted<br />&#32;<strong>Date:</strong
          >&#32;2026-11-26<br />&#32;<strong>Superseded in part by:</strong
          >&#32;<a
            href="/governance/decisions/0014-author-normative-documents-as-markdown-in-the-website-repository"
            >Governance decision 0014</a
          >
        </p>
        <div class="doc-note" role="note">
          <p class="doc-note-label">
            <svg
              class="doc-note-icon"
              width="16"
              height="16"
              viewBox="0 0 16 16"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
              focusable="false"
            >
              <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.25"></circle>
              <path d="M8 7.25v3.75"></path>
              <path d="M8 5.1h.01"></path>
            </svg>
            <span>Note</span>
          </p>
          <p>
            <a
              href="/governance/decisions/0014-author-normative-documents-as-markdown-in-the-website-repository"
              >Governance decision 0014</a
            >&#32;supersedes the alternative in the section Context that begins
            &quot;Keep Markdown as the source and render it in the website&quot;
            and the sentence in the section Decision that begins &quot;Each
            document has one canonical page, written directly as an Astro
            page&quot;. The rest of this decision remains in force.
          </p>
        </div>
        <h2 id="context">Context</h2>
        <p>
          Macro Evidence keeps its organization-level governance in a standalone
          repository,&#32;<code>macro-evidence/governance</code>. That
          repository holds the organization charter, the governance and
          decision-making charter, the documentation standards, the contribution
          policy, the trademarks policy, the contributor-agreement forms and
          process documents, and the organization-level decision records.
          Readers reach these records on GitHub. The organization website
          at&#32;<code>macro-evidence.com</code>&#32;summarizes them and links
          out to them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Website decision 0005 chose that arrangement. It evaluated a pinned
          mirror of governance records inside the website and rejected it. The
          mirror duplicated a large document corpus, needed synchronization and
          Markdown-publication machinery, and gave a repository-document reading
          experience inside pages designed as an institutional website.
        </p>
        <p>
          Governance exists to govern Macro Evidence. It is not an independent
          software product with its own contributor community or release
          lifecycle. A separate repository adds a second repository to
          administer, a second set of settings and contributor entry points, a
          cross-repository link for every policy reference, and a reading
          experience that leaves the organization's own domain.
        </p>
        <p>Three alternatives were considered:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Keep two repositories and improve the links. This keeps the separate
            administration and the departure from the organization's domain.
          </li>
          <li>
            Mirror the governance Markdown into the website. Website decision
            0005 rejected this approach, and the reasons still apply.
          </li>
          <li>
            Keep Markdown as the source and render it in the website. This keeps
            two authoring formats for one published page and adds a rendering
            layer whose only purpose is to preserve the old format.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The chosen option has no mirror, no second source and no rendering
          layer. The published pages are the canonical text.
        </p>
        <h2 id="decision">Decision</h2>
        <p>
          Make&#32;<code>macro-evidence/website</code>&#32;the canonical source
          of Macro Evidence's public governance content. Publish that content as
          first-party pages on&#32;<code>macro-evidence.com</code>. Retire the
          standalone&#32;<code>governance</code>&#32;repository after the
          conditions below are met.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why a separate repository is no longer needed.</strong
          >&#32;Governance documents change rarely. They are maintained and
          reviewed through the same process as the website. None of them needs
          its own release process or contributor community. One repository is
          simpler to administer and to verify.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why the website repository.</strong>&#32;The website is the
          organization's first-party institutional surface. Publishing
          governance there lets Macro Evidence direct readers to its own domain
          for its charter, governance, contribution policy, trademarks, legal
          material and decisions. Each document has one canonical public
          address.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Publication and infrastructure.</strong>&#32;Public
          institutional publication and source-control infrastructure are
          separate functions. The website repository carries the published
          institutional documents. The&#32;<code>macro-evidence/.github</code
          >&#32;repository continues to carry GitHub-specific infrastructure:
          community-health files, issue and pull request templates, the
          organization profile and the reusable workflows. This decision does
          not move&#32;<code>.github</code>&#32;into the website repository.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Code of Conduct is published at&#32;<code>/code-of-conduct</code>.
          A complete distribution copy remains in&#32;<code>.github</code
          >&#32;for GitHub's community-health surface. The website page is the
          canonical reading version. Both copies change together when the policy
          changes. The Contributor Covenant attribution and the credit to
          Mozilla's code of conduct team stay with both.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ownership and page structure.</strong>&#32;Each document has
          one canonical page, written directly as an Astro page in the website
          repository. There is no separate Markdown corpus, content repository,
          mirror or synchronization mechanism.
        </p>
        <div
          class="table-scroll"
          role="region"
          tabindex="0"
          aria-label="Table, Decision"
        >
          <table>
            <thead
              ><tr
                ><th>Document</th>
                <th>Canonical address</th></tr
              ></thead
            ><tbody
              ><tr
                ><td>Organization &amp; Platform Charter</td>
                <td><code>/governance/organization-charter</code></td></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Governance &amp; Decision-Making Charter</td>
                <td><code>/governance/decision-making</code></td></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Documentation Standards</td>
                <td><code>/governance/documentation-standards</code></td></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Contribution Policy</td>
                <td><code>/contribute</code></td></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Trademarks Policy</td>
                <td><code>/trademarks</code></td></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Code of Conduct</td>
                <td><code>/code-of-conduct</code></td></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Governance decisions</td>
                <td
                  ><code>/governance/decisions</code>, with one page for each
                  decision</td
                ></tr
              >
              <tr
                ><td>Contributor agreements</td>
                <td
                  ><code>/legal/contributor-agreements</code>&#32;and the pages
                  below it</td
                ></tr
              ></tbody
            >
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The&#32;<code>/governance</code>&#32;page remains the entry point and
          orientation page for governance. The&#32;<code>/about</code>&#32;page
          remains an orientation page. No full policy text appears on more than
          one page.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Existing policies and decisions.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The twelve Governance decisions keep their numbers, titles, dates,
            statuses, reasoning and successor relationships. Superseded
            decisions stay visibly superseded. A statement that describes the
            former repository arrangement is historical and stays as written.
            Only broken or outdated links, factual errors and necessary location
            references are corrected.
          </li>
          <li>
            The five foundational policy documents continue in substance without
            interruption. Each is re-established on the website at version
            1.0.0. This is a one-time re-baseline for the new publication home.
            It is not a MAJOR release. Earlier changelog entries are not carried
            over. The release date of a re-established document is the date its
            page merges into the&#32;<code>main</code>&#32;branch of the website
            repository.
          </li>
          <li>
            A re-established document differs from its earlier published version
            only in repository and location references, in the decision-series
            language below, and in the addition of the&#32;<code>ci</code
            >&#32;commit type to the supported list. A correction beyond these
            is stated in the changelog entry of the affected document.
          </li>
          <li>
            The contributor-agreement documents keep their versions, status and
            wording.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Decision series.</strong>&#32;Two decision series exist in the
          website repository. The Governance series holds organization-level
          decisions and is published under&#32;<code>/governance/decisions</code
          >. The Website series holds technical and public-interface decisions
          specific to the website and stays in the&#32;<code>decisions/</code
          >&#32;directory as Markdown files. Each series keeps its own numbering
          and its own index. No series is renumbered. A decision is cited with
          its series, for example Governance decision 0013 or Website decision
          0005. Documentation Standards and the Governance &amp; Decision-Making
          Charter state this rule.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Maintenance and contribution boundary.</strong>&#32;Macro
          Evidence maintains the governance pages through the website
          repository. A change to a governance page follows the Contribution
          Policy, the documentation standards and the website verification.
          External suggestions are welcome as proposals. Acceptance and
          authority remain with Macro Evidence. The merge requirement for
          external copyrightable contributions applies to governance content in
          the website repository. This decision does not change the Contribution
          Policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Licensing and legal documents.</strong>&#32;The website
          repository keeps Apache-2.0 for software and CC BY-SA 4.0 for
          human-readable institutional content. Official identity assets and
          third-party material keep their separate terms. The Individual and
          Entity Contributor License Agreement reference forms are adapted from
          Harmony Contributor License Agreement Version 1.0. They remain under
          CC BY 3.0 with their attribution and provenance notices, and&#32;<code
            >LICENSING.md</code
          >&#32;records this exception at file level. The Apache-2.0 grant is
          not applied to legal-form text because that text sits in a source
          file. Publishing the forms as pages changes no operative wording,
          version or provenance. The forms stay public reference forms and are
          not executed agreements. Executed agreements, signatures, private
          contact details, entity schedules and contributor registers are never
          published and never stored in the repository. The hash and
          execution-record practices of the contributor-agreement process are
          unchanged.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Link migration.</strong>&#32;Every reference to the&#32;<code
            >governance</code
          >&#32;repository in the controlled Macro Evidence repositories is
          inventoried and classified. Each reference is corrected, kept
          deliberately, or removed with a stated reason. Readers of policy text
          are directed to first-party addresses. A reference that records a
          historical fact, such as a decision that describes the former
          repository arrangement, stays as written. Where a source-code link
          remains useful, it points to the website repository and its current
          path.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Retirement of the Governance repository.</strong
          >&#32;The&#32;<code>governance</code>&#32;repository is retired by
          deletion. It does not become a public archive or a second maintained
          source. Retirement happens only when all of these hold:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>all necessary content is published on the website;</li>
          <li>all organization-level decisions are checked;</li>
          <li>licensing and privacy boundaries are verified;</li>
          <li>every controlled repository is inspected and updated;</li>
          <li>the generated public routes work;</li>
          <li>the deployment is accepted;</li>
          <li>no active reference requires the old repository; and</li>
          <li>the maintainer gives separate final approval.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A temporary recovery export may be kept as a safety measure. It does
          not enter the website repository and is not published. Its retention
          and disposal are decided before deletion.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Transition.</strong>&#32;Until a document is published on the
          production site at its address, the copy in the&#32;<code
            >governance</code
          >&#32;repository remains the canonical text for that document. When
          the page is published, the website page becomes canonical and the
          former copy is no longer maintained.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Relation to earlier decisions.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            This decision supersedes Website decision 0005. The principle that
            records owned by another repository stay there carries forward for
            records that remain elsewhere, such as the community-health files
            and the security-reporting policy file in&#32;<code
              >macro-evidence/.github</code
            >&#32;and the technical documentation in&#32;<code
              >macro-evidence/macro-data-observatory</code
            >. The website links to those records and does not mirror them.
          </li>
          <li>
            This decision narrows Website decision 0001 and Website decision
            0003 where they say that canonical governance, policy and
            contribution records stay in the repositories that own them and that
            the website links to them. For the governance content named above,
            the website repository is the owner and the website publishes the
            full text.
          </li>
          <li>
            Governance decisions 0001 to 0012 stay in force with their recorded
            statuses. Governance decision 0012 and the&#32;<code>.github</code
            >&#32;workflows are not changed.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h2 id="consequences">Consequences</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Macro Evidence administers one repository fewer: one set of
            settings, rulesets, merge rules, advisories and contributor entry
            points.
          </li>
          <li>
            Each policy has one address on the organization's own domain.
            Readers do not need to leave the domain to read the organization's
            governance.
          </li>
          <li>
            Governance changes ship through the website release and share its
            deployment boundary. A defect in one can delay the other.
          </li>
          <li>
            Policy changes become changes to page source. The pages must be
            verified at phone, tablet and desktop widths, and the verification
            scripts must list every route.
          </li>
          <li>
            The Git history of the&#32;<code>governance</code>&#32;repository is
            not imported. After retirement, earlier versions of the policies and
            that repository's commit history are no longer published. The
            decision record, its numbering and its statuses preserve the
            decision history.
          </li>
          <li>
            Version numbers reset for the five re-established documents. A
            citation of an earlier version number, such as Charter 1.3.1, refers
            to the retired publication.
          </li>
          <li>
            A route that the verification scripts do not know fails the release
            verification. This is intended.
          </li>
          <li>
            A material change to this publication model needs a new decision.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <div class="reference-links">
        <a class="text-link" href="/governance/decisions">
          All Governance decisions →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
